  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 250 PM Friday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1... Dangerous heat and humidity will remain in place \nacross portions of NC tomorrow through early next week. \n\nEarly afternoon satellite data reveals convection crossing the \nmountains into the Mid Atlantic. Varying amounts of mid/high clouds \nhave been present across central NC through much of the day but \ngiven mild morning low temps, there has been no problem seeing heat \nindex values climb above 100 (before noon in many spots).\n\nThe Heat Advisory will remain in place until 8 pm tonight. The \ncombination of loss of daytime heating plus the potential for rain \ncooled air from showers and storms should bring some degree of \nrelief by this evening. Mid level heights and thicknesses remain \nrelatively similar across the southern half of our forecast area on \nSaturday. As such, temps and dewpoints should yield similar heat \nindex values along and south of a Stanly to Wayne Co line, and a \nHeat Advisory will be in effect from 11a-8p tomorrow as well. \n\nWith a stalled front lingering across the area and little in the way \nof backdoor cold air to cleanly push it through our area, \ndangerously hot heat index values can be expected across portions of \nthe area Sunday and Monday and additional Heat Advisories may be \nneeded through early next week. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... There is a Marginal risk of severe thunderstorms \nthrough this evening areawide, and a Marginal to Slight risk of \nsevere thunderstorms Saturday with the higher risk over western and \nsouthern areas of central NC. Damaging winds are the primary threat.\n\nThe latest surface analysis shows the primary synoptic frontal zone \nfrom the Midwest to the Mid Atlantic, with a trough trailing from DE \nS and SW through central NC. A series of old outflows along and S of \nthis front generated from decaying/decayed MCSs are already spawning \nconvection over NC, with storms having started over the E slopes of \nthe S Appalachians and Foothills drifting SE into the W Piedmont, \nwhile another broken band of storms is tracking from central WV into \nE KY, also pushing SE. These boundaries will continue to track SE \nover VA/NC through this evening, with additional scattered \nconvection over our area feeding on high PW (130-140% of normal) and \n3000-4000 J/kg of SBCAPE, yielding a wet microburst threat with the \ndeeper convection, although poor deep layer shear should limit storm \norganization. Coverage should be higher over the N than the S, given \nthe mid level ridging and lower mid level lapse rates further S, but \na stray shower or storm can't be ruled out anywhere given the strong \nheating, high moisture, and favorable instability. Activity should \ndwindle gradually during the early overnight hours.\n\nModels continue to favor a substantial outflow passage SSW through \nmuch of central NC tonight into early Sat, while the synoptic front \nremains well to our N, aligned vertically with the stronger WNW mid \nlevel steering from the Midwest to the Mid Atlantic. This diffuse \nboundary is expected to set up over central NC Sat roughly from the \nTriad region through the central Coastal Plain, and N of this front \nwill see higher low-level stability and a pool of sct-bkn morning \nstratus, which should help hold temps down a bit from today's \nreadings. But along and south of this boundary, ample heating and \nvery warm/moist air should support another round of moderate to high \nafternoon SBCAPE amidst continued-high PW, thus isold to sctd storms \nare again expected, with another primary threat from damaging \nstraight-line winds, despite borderline lapse rates aloft and poor \ndeep layer bulk shear. \n\nAs we'll stay within this steamy air mass into at least early next \nweek with hot temps, high humidity, and above-normal PW, isolated to \nscattered afternoon/evening showers will remain possible. Most \nmodels show the mid level ridge gradually flattening, sinking \nsouthward and gradually retrograding to across the S Plains and Gulf \nCoast through the middle of next week, with another uptick in WNW \nmid level flow over our area. Thus, scattered diurnal-favored \nshowers and storms will remain possible through mid week, although \nnot every place will see activity every day.",
